"A leaf floated on the surface of the water.": "b535c1d93c72",
"A tsunami can cross an ocean in a few hours.": "8c535e19a346",
"A tsunami is not made by the wind. Most tsunamis begin with a great earthquake under the sea. If the seabed suddenly jumps up or drops down, it lifts or lowers all the water above it, from the bottom of the sea to the top. That huge push spreads outward in every direction. A landslide under the sea or a volcano erupting in the sea can do the same thing.": "b966d4391bcb",
"Everything changes near the land. The water becomes shallow, so the front of the tsunami slows down. The water behind it is still in deeper water and still moving fast. It catches up and piles on top. The tsunami grows from something a ship cannot feel into a wall of water.": "41903e483edd",
"Out in the deep ocean, a tsunami is hard to notice. It may be less than a meter high, and the distance from one crest to the next can be hundreds of kilometers, so a ship simply rises and falls a little. But it is moving very fast. Its speed depends on the depth of the water. In the open ocean it travels 500 to 1,000 kilometers an hour, about as fast as a jet plane.": "145ba0bb76a8",
"Sometimes the sea gives a warning. Before a tsunami arrives, the water may suddenly pull far back from the shore and leave the seabed bare. This does not always happen. So the rule is simple. If you are near the sea and you feel a strong earthquake, or you see the water draw back, or you hear a tsunami warning, do not wait and do not watch. Go to high ground at once.": "cc42463b90df",
"Stand on a beach and watch the waves. Each one rises, curls over, breaks, and slides back. A minute later you have seen ten of them. These waves are made by the wind blowing across the sea. They look strong, but they move only the water near the top. A diver a little way down hardly feels them.": "77170c14f8bc",
"Stand on a beach and watch the waves. Each one rises, curls over, breaks, and slides back. A minute later you have seen ten of them. These waves are made by the wind blowing across the sea. They look strong, but they move only the water near the top. A diver a little way down hardly feels them. A tsunami is not made by the wind. Most tsunamis begin with a great earthquake under the sea. If the seabed suddenly jumps up or drops down, it lifts or lowers all the water above it, from the bottom of the sea to the top. That huge push spreads outward in every direction. A landslide under the sea or a volcano erupting in the sea can do the same thing. Out in the deep ocean, a tsunami is hard to notice. It may be less than a meter high, and the distance from one crest to the next can be hundreds of kilometers, so a ship simply rises and falls a little. But it is moving very fast. Its speed depends on the depth of the water. In the open ocean it travels 500 to 1,000 kilometers an hour, about as fast as a jet plane. Everything changes near the land. The water becomes shallow, so the front of the tsunami slows down. The water behind it is still in deeper water and still moving fast. It catches up and piles on top. The tsunami grows from something a ship cannot feel into a wall of water. When it reaches the shore, a tsunami does not behave like a beach wave. It does not break and slide back in a few seconds. The whole sea keeps pouring onto the land for minutes, and it carries boats, cars, and pieces of buildings with it. Then it drains back out, just as strongly. There is usually more than one wave. Sometimes the sea gives a warning. Before a tsunami arrives, the water may suddenly pull far back from the shore and leave the seabed bare. This does not always happen. So the rule is simple. If you are near the sea and you feel a strong earthquake, or you see the water draw back, or you hear a tsunami warning, do not wait and do not watch. Go to high ground at once.": "043debfe514f",
